entry.summary:
So loops in ruby has several ways to implement 10.times do |i| puts i end while condition # ... end users.each do |user| # ... end until finished # ... end
Go looks at all of this and basically says: We only need one loop. That loop is for. Before getting into loops, though, let's look at conditions. Go has the familiar if, else if, and else. if age >= 18 { fmt.Println("Adult") } else { fmt.Println("Minor") }
The syntax is pretty close to Ruby, but there is one important difference. Go does not use end. Ruby: if age >= 18 puts "Adult" else puts "Minor" end
Go uses curly braces to define the block: if age >= 18 { fmt.Println("Adult") } else { fmt.Println("Minor") }
Coming from Ruby, I had to get used to seeing {} everywhere. The familiar operators are there: …
